<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The April 2026 PPI reading of 6.0% year-over-year is the highest since December 2022, but the chart illustrates a critical structural difference: the current spike is concentrated in a brief supply-shock window tied to the Iran war, whereas the 2021-2022 cycle sustained double-digit readings for fourteen consecutive months. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics.</p>

<p>What makes the current situation behaviourally dangerous is not that the numbers are alarming, but that they are alarming in a sequence that rhymes with a prior traumatic experience. Terrance Odean and Brad Barber''s 1999 research on individual investor trading behaviour established that investors trade most actively, and most destructively, precisely when information flow is highest. Two major data releases in 48 hours, both above consensus, constitute exactly the kind of information environment that drives reactive portfolio decisions.</p>

<h2>Why the Pattern-Match to 2022 Is Misleading</h2>

<p>The 2021-2022 inflation cycle was demand-driven and supply-constrained simultaneously: fiscal stimulus had flooded the economy with purchasing power precisely when global supply chains were fractured. The Federal Reserve held rates near zero for eighteen months into that dynamic before beginning one of the most aggressive tightening cycles in its history. The damage to balanced portfolios was severe and prolonged.</p>

<p>The 2026 inflation episode has a different architecture. It is an energy supply shock caused by a specific, identifiable, and in principle resolvable geopolitical event: the closure of the Strait of Hormuz beginning in late February. The Bank of Canada''s April 29 Monetary Policy Report acknowledged this directly, noting that "so far, there is little evidence that higher oil prices have fed through to other goods and services prices more broadly." Long-term inflation expectations in Canada remain anchored. The IEA has characterized the disruption as the largest single supply event in the history of the oil market, but supply shocks, unlike demand shocks, carry the seeds of their own reversal: they end when the cause ends.</p>

<p>That distinction matters to the portfolio decision, but it does not feel that way when the data is arriving. Richard Thaler''s work on mental accounting suggests that investors treat each data event as a discrete unit rather than as part of a longer pattern, which is exactly why sequential shocks feel more confirming than they analytically are. The investor who knows intellectually that this is a supply shock may still feel, viscerally, that it is 2022 again.</p>

<h2>The Specific Population of At-Risk Clients</h2>

<p>Not all clients are equally susceptible to the current environment. The availability heuristic operates most powerfully on those with vivid direct experience of the reference event. Clients who watched their balanced portfolios fall 15% to 20% in 2022 carry a specific emotional memory that the current data sequence is actively reactivating. Clients who are newer to investing, or who held heavily defensive portfolios through 2022, carry a different emotional baseline and are less likely to be pattern-matching to that episode.</p>

<p>Clients approaching or in early retirement are particularly vulnerable for a compounding reason: the combination of inflation anxiety and sequence-of-returns awareness creates a powerful motivation to reduce equity exposure at precisely the wrong moment. Daniel Kahneman''s loss aversion finding, that losses are felt approximately twice as intensely as equivalent gains, means that the prospect of further inflation-driven market pressure feels far worse than the data alone would suggest it should.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who lived through 2022 are experiencing something closer to dread than simple worry. Two consecutive above-consensus inflation prints are reactivating a specific memory of a prolonged, damaging inflation cycle. Clients in or near retirement feel this most acutely because their exposure to sequence-of-returns risk is highest. Clients who are more recently invested may be anxious but do not carry the same visceral reference point. The emotional state is pattern recognition triggering alarm, not necessarily a rational assessment of the current data.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to talk about the inflation numbers that came out this week, because I know they look alarming and I want to give you some honest context before we decide whether they change anything about your plan.

The PPI and CPI prints were genuinely above expectations. I am not going to pretend otherwise. But there is a specific reason they are this high right now, and it is different from what drove inflation in 2021 and 2022.

What we are seeing is an energy shock caused by the closure of the Strait of Hormuz. That is a real event with a real cause. The Bank of Canada noted two weeks ago that outside of energy, prices are not spreading the way they did in the last cycle. Long-term inflation expectations in Canada are still anchored. That matters because it means the policy response is likely to be different this time.

In 2022, the problem was everywhere and the cause was not going to resolve on its own. This time, the cause is specific and identifiable. That does not make it painless, but it changes the probability distribution for what comes next.

Your plan was built to handle periods like this. What would need to be true for you to feel comfortable staying with it through the next few months?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients in drawdown who experienced 2022 portfolio losses and are now pattern-matching to that episode. Clients with inflation-linked liabilities such as near-term real estate purchases or business cost exposure.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Accumulators in balanced portfolios who understand the supply-shock dynamic but are anxious about headline numbers. Clients with significant fixed-income exposure watching yields respond to the inflation data.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with significant Canadian energy exposure, where high oil prices translate directly to earnings support. Clients holding inflation-protected instruments such as real return bonds or commodity-linked funds.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Segment your client list by 2022 drawdown experience: those who saw the largest losses then are the highest-risk group for reactive decisions now.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review fixed-income duration exposure across accounts. If clients are holding long-duration bond funds, the inflation data increases the case for a duration conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients in or within three years of retirement who hold above-average equity allocations. This is the specific intersection of loss aversion and sequence-of-returns risk.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document any client conversations about portfolio changes prompted by this week''s data. Behavioral coaching conversations should be on file.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a brief written summary of the supply-shock vs. demand-shock distinction. Many clients respond better to a written explanation they can return to than a single phone call.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The inflation numbers this week, and what they actually mean for your plan<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to follow up on our conversation with a few thoughts in writing, because I find these topics easier to absorb when you can come back to them.<br><br>
The U.S. inflation data released Tuesday and Wednesday was above expectations. The consumer price index came in at 3.8% year-over-year, and the producer price index came in at 6.0%. Both prints were higher than analysts had forecast.<br><br>
Here is the important context: the dominant driver of both numbers is energy prices, which have surged since the closure of the Strait of Hormuz in late February. The Bank of Canada, in its most recent assessment on April 29, noted that outside of energy, there is little evidence of broader price pressures spreading through the Canadian economy the way they did in 2021 and 2022. Long-term inflation expectations remain anchored.<br><br>
That does not mean there is no uncertainty ahead. But the structure of this inflation episode is meaningfully different from the last one, and your plan was designed to handle exactly these kinds of periods of elevated uncertainty.<br><br>
If you have questions or want to talk through any part of this, I am available.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors monitoring inflation headlines:</strong> Self-directed investors with no advisor are making reactive decisions based on the same data flow their advised counterparts are receiving, but without a framework. They are the most likely population to be drawing the wrong 2022 analogy.</p>
<p><strong>Recently retired individuals managing their own portfolios:</strong> The intersection of loss aversion, sequence-of-returns risk, and inflation anxiety makes recently retired DIY investors particularly exposed to poor decision-making in this environment.</p>
<p><strong>High-net-worth business owners with energy exposure:</strong> This client type tends to be highly engaged with inflation data because it affects their operating costs directly. An advisor who can distinguish supply shock from structural inflation earns credibility here.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because we had two major inflation releases this week, and I am reaching out to people who I think could use a clear-headed framework for what it means versus what 2022 was, because the two situations look similar on the surface but are actually quite different.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The advisor who understands behavioural finance has a specific advantage this week: the ability to separate the signal from the emotional noise. Inflation data that triggers the 2022 memory is causing many investors to consider portfolio changes that the underlying analysis does not support. An advisor who can articulate clearly why this episode is structurally different, and who has a documented plan for navigating it, is demonstrating the most important thing an advisor can demonstrate in a volatile period: that they have a framework, not just an opinion.</p>
<p>The prospecting window here is broad. Any investor managing their own money right now is experiencing the same information overload as advised clients, but without anyone to call.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How are you thinking about the inflation data that came out this week?</p>
<p>Does it remind you of anything you experienced before, and how did you handle your portfolio then?</p>
<p>When you are making decisions about your portfolio right now, how much of that is based on a written plan versus your read on current conditions?</p>
<p>If inflation stays elevated for the next six months, what does that mean for the financial decisions you are facing right now?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Two inflation prints in 48 hours, and why this is not 2022<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
I am reaching out because of the inflation data that came out this week. The U.S. CPI and PPI both came in above expectations, and I have been talking to a number of people this week who are drawing the comparison to 2021-2022 and wondering if they should be making changes to their portfolios.<br><br>
I wanted to share a perspective that I think is worth considering: the current inflation episode is structurally different from the last one. The primary driver right now is an energy supply shock caused by the Strait of Hormuz closure. The Bank of Canada explicitly noted that broader price pressures are not spreading the way they did in the last cycle. That is a meaningful distinction for portfolio decision-making.<br><br>
If you are managing your own investments right now and want to talk through the framework I use for navigating this kind of environment, I would be glad to make time for a conversation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
