<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The corporate and trust structure bears the highest federal tax cost on a $300,000 capital gain at the current two-thirds inclusion rate: $66,000 in federal tax before provincial rates. The personal non-registered account benefits from the $250,000 individual threshold, reducing the federal bill to approximately $52,250 on the same gain. Source: Canada Revenue Agency.</p>

<h2>The TFSA Angle Most Advisors Are Missing</h2>

<p>For clients who hold appreciated energy names in non-registered accounts and have unused TFSA contribution room, the mechanics are straightforward but the execution requires care. A client who contributes appreciated shares in-kind to a TFSA triggers a deemed disposition at fair market value on the date of contribution. That deemed disposition realizes the capital gain at the current inclusion rate. After transfer, all future growth inside the TFSA is sheltered.</p>

<p>The question for each client is whether the tax cost of the deemed disposition today is worth the shelter on future appreciation. For clients with energy positions where the gain is large and where further appreciation is plausible (WTI remaining elevated through a prolonged Strait of Hormuz disruption is one scenario), contributing in-kind can be the correct call. But it requires knowing the adjusted cost base precisely, calculating the deemed disposition tax, and confirming that the client has adequate TFSA room. The 2026 TFSA contribution limit is $7,000, with cumulative room for eligible Canadians who have never contributed potentially exceeding $100,000.</p>

<h2>The Corporate Account Calculus</h2>

<p>For incorporated professionals and business owners, the analysis is more compressed. There is no $250,000 annual threshold in a corporation. The two-thirds inclusion rate applies from dollar one, and at a combined federal and provincial corporate marginal tax rate, the after-tax cost of realizing a large energy gain inside the corporation is substantially higher than realizing it personally.</p>

<p>This does not mean the answer is always to pay the tax now. A corporation that holds an appreciated energy position has the option of waiting for a more favourable environment, and the capital dividend account mechanism allows a portion of the gain to flow to shareholders tax-free. But with WTI above $100 and the inclusion rate debate resolved, the argument for deferral is weaker than it was in 2024 when the rate change was still uncertain. The planning window is open, but it requires a specific conversation about each client''s corporate structure, existing capital dividend account balance, and expectations for the energy position going forward.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with large energy positions are experiencing two competing emotions: satisfaction at the appreciation and anxiety about whether they should be acting on it before something changes. Incorporated business owners feel this most acutely because they are accustomed to making active tax decisions and the two-thirds inclusion rate in their corporation gives them a concrete number to react to. Clients in non-registered accounts are more likely to be paralyzed by uncertainty about what the right move is.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Your energy holdings have done well in this environment, and that creates a genuine planning question we should walk through together.

The capital gains inclusion rate is settled for now. Two-thirds applies to all corporate gains, and to personal gains above $250,000 annually. So the first thing I want to do is make sure we know the adjusted cost base on each position and understand what the tax bill would actually look like in each scenario.

The second question is where those positions sit. Holdings in a TFSA are sheltered from any tax on sale. Holdings in a non-registered personal account benefit from the $250,000 threshold. Holdings inside your corporation face the two-thirds rate from the first dollar.

There is also a TFSA opportunity worth looking at. If you have unused contribution room and you hold appreciated energy shares outside registered accounts, we can move them in-kind. That triggers the gain now, but everything that grows inside the TFSA after that point is permanently sheltered.

I want to put together a specific analysis for your situation before we make any moves. Can we set up a working session this week?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Incorporated professionals and business owners holding appreciated Canadian energy or commodity producers inside a corporation. The two-thirds inclusion from dollar one makes timing decisions more consequential.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Individual investors in non-registered accounts with energy gains below the $250,000 annual threshold who are weighing whether to realize gains or hold for further appreciation.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with unused TFSA room who can shelter future energy-sector appreciation by contributing appreciated shares in-kind and accepting the current deemed disposition.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify all clients with significant Canadian energy or commodity positions held outside registered accounts, or inside corporate investment portfolios.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull adjusted cost base data for each position and calculate the approximate capital gain at current prices.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag corporate account holders for priority review: two-thirds inclusion from dollar one creates the most pressing planning conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review TFSA contribution room for clients with non-registered energy gains: the in-kind contribution analysis is a high-value conversation right now.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm capital dividend account balances for incorporated clients before any realized gain conversation: the CDA affects the after-tax cost of distribution.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your energy positions and the capital gains planning question<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to follow up on our conversation about your energy holdings. With oil prices elevated and your positions showing meaningful appreciation, it is worth taking a structured look at the tax implications before we decide whether to act.<br><br>
The key facts for your situation: the capital gains inclusion rate is two-thirds on corporate gains from the first dollar, and two-thirds on personal gains above $250,000 annually. Gains inside a TFSA remain tax-free on sale.<br><br>
I am putting together a specific analysis of your positions, their adjusted cost bases, and the approximate tax impact of realizing under each scenario. I will also look at your TFSA contribution room to see whether an in-kind contribution makes sense for any of the non-registered holdings.<br><br>
I will have that to you by the end of the week. In the meantime, please let me know if anything has changed in your plans for these positions.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Incorporated professionals with investment portfolios:</strong> Physicians, lawyers, engineers, and other incorporated professionals who hold investment portfolios inside their corporations are the highest-priority prospect in this environment. The two-thirds inclusion rate on all corporate gains creates an urgent planning conversation they may not be having with their current advisor.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors with energy-heavy non-registered accounts:</strong> Self-directed investors who have watched energy names appreciate since the Hormuz closure are increasingly likely to be wondering whether they should sell and when. An advisor who can run the actual tax math is demonstrating concrete value.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners approaching retirement:</strong> The combination of appreciated corporate investments, a capital dividend account, and a potential business sale creates multi-dimensional tax planning complexity that generalist advisors often underserve.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am reaching out because with oil above $100, a lot of Canadian investors are sitting on meaningful energy gains right now, and the tax decision is more complex than it looks, especially if you hold any of those positions inside a corporation or in a non-registered account above the $250,000 threshold.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The capital gains planning conversation requires knowing four things simultaneously: where the positions sit, what the adjusted cost base is, what the inclusion rate calculation looks like across account types, and whether a TFSA in-kind contribution changes the analysis. Most DIY investors do not have all four of these in front of them. An advisor who can present a clear, account-specific analysis is providing something that cannot be found on a brokerage platform or a financial news site.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you hold any Canadian energy names or commodity producers in a non-registered account or inside a corporation?</p>
<p>Do you know the adjusted cost base on those positions?</p>
<p>Have you thought about whether it makes sense to realize some of those gains now, or are you planning to hold through the current oil price environment?</p>
<p>Do you have unused TFSA contribution room that you have not yet put to work?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Oil above $100 and the capital gains question Canadian investors need to answer<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
With WTI crude above $100, many Canadian investors are sitting on meaningful unrealized gains in energy and commodity positions. That creates a specific tax planning question that is worth working through carefully, because the answer depends on where those positions are held.<br><br>
The capital gains inclusion rate is currently two-thirds on all corporate gains and on personal gains above $250,000 annually. If you hold appreciated energy positions inside a corporation, the tax calculus is different from holding them in a personal non-registered account, and different again from what sits inside a TFSA.<br><br>
If you would like to talk through your specific situation, I am glad to run the analysis. There is no cost to the conversation, and in my experience, the account-type question alone often changes the decision considerably.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
