Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
With the confirmation of Kevin Warsh as Federal Reserve chair and two consecutive U.S. inflation surprises this week, the rate outlook has changed in a specific way that I think is worth a clear-headed conversation.<br><br>
The Bank of Canada meets on June 10. The Fed meets June 16-17. The most likely outcome at both meetings is a hold, and the probability of a cut by the end of 2026 has declined meaningfully in the past 48 hours. If you have a mortgage renewal, a GIC maturing, or a fixed-income portfolio that was positioned for falling rates, this shift matters for your planning.<br><br>
I work with clients specifically on how to position for different rate scenarios, and I would be glad to offer a no-obligation conversation about what the current picture means for your situation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
