<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI peaked near $117 in early April before the ceasefire announcement triggered a sharp pullback. The subsequent reimposition of the U.S. naval blockade and the failure of the Islamabad negotiations have kept prices well above $100. The IEA''s Wednesday warning that the market will remain undersupplied until October even with a resolution next month sets the floor for the current range. Source: Trading Economics, NYMEX.</p>

<h2>The Trump-Xi Variable</h2>

<p>U.S. President Trump is in Beijing this week for a summit with Chinese President Xi Jinping. The meeting agenda includes bilateral trade, artificial intelligence policy, and U.S. arms sales to Taiwan. The Strait of Hormuz crisis is not formally on the agenda, but it is structurally unavoidable: China receives approximately one-third of its crude oil through the strait. The disruption has cost China more than any other single country in terms of supply chain stress and oil import cost.</p>

<p>China''s role in any Hormuz resolution has two dimensions. The first is diplomatic: Beijing has maintained back-channel communications with Tehran throughout the conflict and is one of the few actors with sufficient economic leverage over Iran to accelerate a negotiated settlement. The second is strategic: China may calculate that U.S. distraction in the Gulf is advantageous and that its own energy security concerns are best managed by alternative supply arrangements rather than by facilitating a U.S.-Iran deal that strengthens American credibility in the region.</p>

<p>For Canadian portfolios, the Trump-Xi summit outcome matters primarily through its effect on oil prices. A credible signal that China would support a Hormuz resolution would likely push WTI back toward $85-90. A summit that produces no progress, or that deteriorates into a new round of trade friction, would remove one of the few diplomatic off-ramps and support prices at current levels or higher.</p>

<h2>The Duration Question and Canadian Energy Exposure</h2>

<p>The IEA''s assertion that the market will remain undersupplied until October, even with a June resolution, is the most consequential number in Wednesday''s energy analysis. It means that even in the most optimistic scenario for the geopolitical situation, Canadian energy producers are operating in a favourable price environment for another five months at minimum. Saudi Arabia''s production at its lowest level since 1990 reflects both the Hormuz disruption to Gulf export infrastructure and the broader OPEC response to the supply shock.</p>

<p>The tail risk, which the IEA did not quantify but the situation implies, is an October resolution that arrives into a market that has spent five months drawing down storage buffers. When the strait reopens at full capacity, the demand for restocking will create its own temporary supply surge before prices normalize. The path of Canadian energy stocks through that transition will depend heavily on the speed of the unwind. Gradual reopening supports prices. Abrupt resolution does not.</p>

<p>The base case for Canadian advisors managing energy-weighted client portfolios remains constructive through summer. The contingency to plan for is the abrupt-resolution scenario, where a surprise ceasefire deal could reprice energy names sharply lower in a short window.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with energy exposure are feeling cautiously optimistic but uncertain about how long it lasts. Clients without energy exposure are watching the TSX underperform the S&P 500 and feeling the familiar Canadian frustration of holding a market heavily weighted toward a sector they may have underweighted. Clients with broader concerns about inflation and the cost of living are connecting the dots between the oil price and their grocery and gas bills in a way that feels personal.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The oil situation is more persistent than many analysts expected in March. The IEA said yesterday that even if the conflict ends next month, the market will be undersupplied until October. That means the energy tailwind for Canadian producers has a longer runway than the ceasefire headlines suggested.

The part that is harder to model is the Trump-Xi summit happening right now in Beijing. China gets a third of its oil through the Strait of Hormuz. That gives them both the motivation and potentially the leverage to push for a resolution. If they signal support for a deal, we could see a sharp drop in oil prices. If the summit produces nothing on the energy front, the current range holds.

For your portfolio specifically, the question is how much energy exposure you want to carry into a situation where the upside is further oil price support but the downside is a sudden resolution. That is the conversation I want to make sure we have before summer.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with significant Canadian energy sector exposure who are approaching a decision about whether to take profits or hold through the summer. The IEA''s October undersupply projection is a data point that materially affects that timing decision.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients in balanced portfolios who hold energy indirectly through broad TSX exposure. The sector weighting in the TSX composite means they have energy beta whether or not they have made an active choice.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who had underweighted energy ahead of the conflict and are now considering whether to add exposure at current prices. The IEA duration analysis provides some analytical support for that conversation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy sector weighting across client portfolios and flag any that are materially above or below the TSX composite weighting of approximately 18-20%.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor Trump-Xi summit outcomes over the next 48-72 hours for any language on the Strait of Hormuz or Iran. A joint statement mentioning the strait would be a significant market signal.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the IEA''s October undersupply forecast as the analytical floor for energy price support. Any client taking profit conversations should reference this timeline.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review any client portfolios with concentrated single-name Canadian energy positions. The abrupt-resolution tail risk argues for some diversification within the sector.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The IEA''s October forecast and what it means for your energy holdings<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to share a key data point from yesterday that I think is relevant to your energy positions.<br><br>
The International Energy Agency reported that the global oil market will likely remain significantly undersupplied until October 2026, even if the Strait of Hormuz conflict is resolved next month. Combined with Saudi Arabia reporting its lowest production level since 1990, this suggests the price support for Canadian energy producers has a longer duration than many had assumed following the April ceasefire announcement.<br><br>
The contingency worth being aware of is the abrupt-resolution scenario: if a surprise diplomatic breakthrough occurs, oil prices could reprice lower quickly. The Trump-Xi summit in Beijing this week is one of the few venues where such a development could emerge.<br><br>
I will be watching the summit closely. If anything significant develops, I will be in touch. In the meantime, if you want to discuss your energy positioning, I am available.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Energy-underweighted DIY investors:</strong> Self-directed investors who held conservative or technology-heavy portfolios going into the conflict have underperformed the TSX significantly this year. Many are now wondering whether it is too late to add energy exposure, and the IEA''s duration analysis gives an advisor a specific, sourced answer to that question.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees holding dividend-paying energy names:</strong> Canadian energy producers have historically been strong dividend payers. High oil prices are supporting both earnings and dividend sustainability. A retiree managing their own income-focused portfolio may not have the analytical framework to assess the Hormuz duration question.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with energy supply cost exposure:</strong> Companies with significant fuel, transportation, or petrochemical input costs are experiencing the Hormuz shock on their operating statements, not just their portfolios. An advisor who can connect the geopolitical analysis to the business owner''s dual exposure, operating cost and investment portfolio, is providing genuinely differentiated value.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because the IEA made an important statement yesterday: even if the Strait of Hormuz conflict is resolved next month, global oil markets will remain undersupplied until October. I think that changes the positioning conversation for Canadian investors in a specific way, and I wanted to share the analysis.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The Hormuz crisis has created a two-track Canadian market: energy names outperforming, rate-sensitive and technology names underperforming. An advisor who can explain the IEA''s duration analysis, the Trump-Xi summit dynamic, and the abrupt-resolution tail risk in a single coherent framework is providing the kind of geopolitical-to-portfolio translation that no discount brokerage platform delivers. The prospect who has been managing their own money through this period without that framework has almost certainly made at least one reactive decision they will want to revisit.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How has your portfolio performed since the conflict began in late February, and do you feel your energy exposure has been about right?</p>
<p>Are you following the Trump-Xi summit this week, and do you have a framework for how a diplomatic breakthrough might affect your holdings?</p>
<p>If oil prices dropped sharply on a surprise ceasefire deal, how would that affect the decisions you are currently making about your portfolio?</p>
<p>Are any of your business operating costs significantly affected by fuel prices, and have you thought about how to hedge that exposure through your investment portfolio?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The IEA says oil markets stay tight until October, and what that means for Canadian investors<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Yesterday, the International Energy Agency reported that global oil markets will remain significantly undersupplied until at least October 2026, even assuming the Strait of Hormuz conflict is resolved next month. Saudi Arabia has simultaneously reported its lowest production level since 1990.<br><br>
For Canadian investors, this is a specific analytical input into a decision many people are wrestling with right now: how much energy exposure to hold, and for how long.<br><br>
There is also a tail risk worth understanding: the Trump-Xi summit in Beijing this week is one of the few diplomatic venues where a surprise Hormuz breakthrough could emerge. If it does, energy prices could reprice sharply lower in a short window.<br><br>
I work with clients on exactly these kinds of geopolitical-to-portfolio translation questions. If you would find it useful to talk through your current positioning, I am glad to make time.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
