<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The TSX Energy sub-index has outperformed the composite by approximately 19 percentage points year-to-date. The gap opened almost entirely after the Hormuz closure on February 28. The energy sub-index peaked near 135 in early April before the ceasefire announcement and has consolidated in the 123-125 range since. Source: TMX Group.</p>

<h2>Gold Miners: The Rate Sensitivity That Energy Does Not Have</h2>

<p>Gold miners fell 1.4% Wednesday in a move that illustrates an important distinction within the TSX materials complex. Gold had been the market''s preferred hedge against geopolitical uncertainty, and the metal itself remains elevated near $4,693 per ounce. But gold mining equities are valued not only on the gold price but on the discount rate applied to their future cash flows. When PPI data reinforces the case for a more restrictive Federal Reserve, gold miners reprice lower even if the gold price itself holds or rises.</p>

<p>Agnico Eagle and Barrick each fell near 2% Wednesday. Equinox Gold dropped 3.6% after announcing an all-stock deal to acquire Orla Mining in a transaction that would create a North American gold producer with an implied market value of approximately $18.5 billion. Orla shares dipped 0.7%. The Equinox-Orla transaction is the largest Canadian gold sector M&A announcement of 2026 and reflects the sector''s confidence in sustained elevated gold prices, even as the equities face short-term rate headwinds.</p>

<h2>The U.S. Divergence and What It Means for Canadian Portfolios</h2>

<p>Wednesday''s market structure, TSX down 0.5% while the S&P 500 gained 0.5% to a record close and the Nasdaq rose 1.2% to a record, captures the core tension in Canadian versus U.S. equity allocation. The S&P 500 and Nasdaq are being driven by large-cap technology names that benefit from AI infrastructure spending: Nvidia, Apple, and Tesla all moved higher on news of the Trump-Xi summit in Beijing, where Jensen Huang of Nvidia and Tim Cook of Apple are accompanying the presidential delegation. Canadian technology has no equivalent leverage to that theme.</p>

<p>For Canadian advisors managing clients with both Canadian and U.S. equity exposure, Wednesday''s session reinforces a pattern that has held since the war began: TSX energy outperforms U.S. energy, but TSX technology significantly underperforms U.S. technology, and the net result is that broad TSX exposure has lagged a diversified U.S. equity position year-to-date ex-energy. The Canadian dollar at 72.98 cents U.S. has provided a partial offset for Canadian investors in unhedged U.S. equity positions, as currency translation adds to U.S. returns when the CAD weakens. That tailwind has been meaningful in 2026.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with TSX-heavy portfolios are watching U.S. indices hit record highs while the TSX lags, and the frustration is familiar. Clients with significant energy exposure feel constructive but are wondering whether to take profits. Clients who hold Canadian technology names are experiencing losses that are now approaching 20% year-to-date on the sector and want to understand whether the story has changed or whether this is temporary underperformance they should hold through.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Wednesday''s session captured the two forces that have been driving the Canadian market since late February in a single day. Energy held up because the IEA confirmed the oil supply disruption has more duration than expected. Technology fell because the inflation data points toward higher-for-longer rates, and software valuations are sensitive to that.

The U.S. market hitting records while the TSX trails is a real divergence, and it is worth understanding why. The S&P 500 and Nasdaq are being driven by AI infrastructure names that Canada does not have significant exposure to. That is a structural difference, not a temporary one.

For your portfolio, the question I want to make sure we are answering is whether your Canadian versus U.S. balance still reflects the thesis you had when we set it up, or whether the geopolitical and sector dynamics of 2026 argue for a different weighting. That is a conversation worth having before the summer.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with overweight Canadian technology exposure who are now sitting on significant year-to-date losses. The sector is down nearly 20% and the headwinds, rate sensitivity and structural AI disadvantage, are not resolving quickly.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients in balanced Canadian equity portfolios who hold both energy and technology through index exposure. The net performance depends on the weighting, but the TSX composite is still up approximately 4% year-to-date, which masks the sector bifurcation.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with unhedged U.S. equity exposure who have benefited from both S&P 500 record performance and CAD weakness providing currency translation gains.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review Canadian technology holdings across client accounts. At down nearly 20% year-to-date, assess whether the thesis for each position remains intact or whether the structural AI headwind argument has changed the outlook materially.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review Canadian versus U.S. equity split for clients who have expressed frustration about TSX underperformance. The divergence is real and sourced in identifiable structural factors, not random variance.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients with unhedged U.S. equity exposure for a currency conversation. CAD at 72.98 cents is providing translation tailwinds that will reverse if oil prices drop on a Hormuz resolution.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review gold miner positions in context of the rate environment. Gold the metal remains a valid geopolitical hedge but gold equities carry additional rate sensitivity that metal exposure does not.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
