<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with Canada-only portfolios:</strong> Self-directed investors who hold predominantly Canadian equities have experienced a highly bifurcated year and may be drawing the wrong conclusions from their overall return. An advisor who can decompose the TSX performance into its energy and technology components, and explain the structural versus cyclical nature of each, is providing analysis that most retail investors do not have access to.</p>
<p><strong>Investors frustrated by TSX versus S&P 500 underperformance:</strong> The record highs in the S&P 500 and Nasdaq on Wednesday will generate headlines. Prospects who are asking why their Canadian portfolio is not keeping up have a specific, answerable question that creates a prospecting opening.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees in drawdown with dividend-focused TSX portfolios:</strong> Energy names support dividends at current oil prices, but technology positions may be creating drag. A portfolio review conversation framed around sector positioning rather than stock picking is lower-risk and higher-value.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because the S&P 500 and Nasdaq both hit record highs Wednesday while the TSX fell, and the reason for that gap is specific and worth understanding, particularly if you are managing a Canadian-heavy portfolio right now.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The TSX is not underperforming because Canada is a worse market. It is underperforming in specific sectors for specific reasons: technology lacks AI infrastructure leverage, gold miners carry rate sensitivity that the metal itself does not, and energy is doing exactly what it should given $100 oil but faces an abrupt-resolution tail risk. An advisor who can deliver that sector-by-sector breakdown, connect it to a client''s actual holdings, and translate it into a concrete portfolio review is providing something no financial news summary assembles. The record U.S. index headlines will generate client questions. The advisor who calls first with a framework, rather than waiting for the client to call with anxiety, is in the stronger position.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How is your portfolio positioned between Canadian and U.S. equities, and has that mix been intentional or has it drifted over time?</p>
<p>Do you have a sense of how much of your Canadian equity exposure is in energy versus technology, and how that has affected your returns this year?</p>
<p>When you see the S&P 500 hitting records while the TSX lags, what is your instinct, to rebalance toward the U.S. or to stay the course in Canada?</p>
<p>Are any of your U.S. equity holdings currency-hedged, and do you have a view on where the Canadian dollar is headed?</p>
</div>
